    "innovation": "By compiling sedimentary 231Pa/230Th proxy records, this paper reveals a spatially coherent picture of western Atlantic circulation changes, identifying a two-step AMOC slowdown during Heinrich Stadial 1 driven first by Eurasian and then by Laurentide ice sheet melting.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "western Atlantic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Abrupt climate changes in the past have been attributed to variations in Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) strength. However, the exact timing and magnitude of past AMOC shifts remain elusive, which continues to limit our understanding of the driving mechanisms of such climate variability. Here we show a consistent signal of the 231Pa/230Th proxy that reveals a spatially coherent picture of western Atlantic circulation changes over the last deglaciation, during abrupt millennial-scale climate transitions. At the onset of deglaciation, we observe an early slowdown of circulation in the western Atlantic from around 19 to 16.5 thousand years ago (ka), consistent with the timing of accelerated Eurasian ice melting.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Abrupt climate changes in the past have been attributed to variations in Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) strength. However, the exact timing and magnitude of past AMOC shifts remain elusive, which continues to limit our understanding of the driving mechanisms of such climate variability. Here we show a consistent signal of the 231Pa/230Th proxy that reveals a spatially coherent picture of western Atlantic circulation changes over the last deglaciation, during abrupt millennial-scale climate transitions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The 14C ventilation records of deep and intermediate water masses in the North Atlantic3,41 are also in agreement with our reconstructions. Together, these data support an overall reduced AMOC rate during HS1 and YD, with resumption during the BA, as previously proposed2.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Deep Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "At present, the active southward advection of North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW), the deep component of AMOC, causes greater export of seawater 231Pa than 230Th from the mid-latitude and low-latitude Atlantic10, giving rise to sedimentary 231Pa/230Th ratios lower than the production ratio (<0.093)12.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Gulf Stream",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This finding is consistent with previous reconstructions of the strengths of Iceland-Scotland Overflow Water (major component of NADW) and Florida Current (part of the Gulf Stream: shallow northward flow with a component that compensates for Atlantic deep-water transport southward).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Iceland-Scotland Overflow Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In conclusion, we found that sedimentary 231Pa/230Th time-series from the West and deep high-latitude North Atlantic provide compelling evidence of coherent changes in deep Atlantic circulation strength coincident in timing with millennial-scale climate changes during the deglacial period (Fig. 2). This finding is consistent with previous reconstructions of the strengths of Iceland-Scotland Overflow Water (major component of NADW) and Florida Current (part of the Gulf Stream: shallow northward flow with a component that compensates for Atlantic deep-water transport southward).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
